f SMP commands to avoid mismatches.\n   - **Error Logging:** Devices should log instances of unsupported command codes for debugging and to detect potential security threats.\n   - **User Notification:** Inform the user of the pairing failure with a clear message indicating a protocol error, prompting them to retry pairing or check device compatibility.\n   - **Security Consideration:** Monitor for repeated invalid commands as they could indicate an attack; implement rate-limiting or temporary blacklisting of offending devices if necessary.\n\n**Answer:**\n\nIn the Bluetooth Security Manager Protocol (SMP), if a device receives an invalid or unsupported command code, it shall respond with a "Pairing Failed" command, setting the reason code to "Command Not Supported" (0x07) as defined in Table 3.7 of the specification. This action aborts the current pairing process, and any subsequent pairing must restart from the Pairing Feature Exchange phase. This behavior ensures protocol integrity by halting communication upon encountering unrecognized commands, which could indicate implementation mismatches or potential security threats. To mitigate such issues, ensure protocol compatibility, log errors for analysis, notify users of failures, and monitor for repeated invalid commands as a security precaution.'}[0m
